WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.570
A cooperative asked us to monitor soil

2
00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.570
moisture across forty hectares.

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.420
There is no Wi-Fi, no mains

4
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.200
power, and the nearest mobile

5
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.080
mast is eight kilometres away.

6
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.000
Which network would you choose?

7
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.180
Today: how publish and subscribe works,

8
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.300
when to use MQTT or CoAP, and the

9
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.540
trade-off between range, data rate and

10
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.800
energy in LoRa and mesh networks.

11
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.140
In MQTT, devices never talk

12
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:31.940
to each other directly.

13
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.430
A sensor publishes to a topic on a broker.

14
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.450
Any client that subscribed to that

15
00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:39.950
topic receives the message.

16
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.420
Senders and receivers stay decoupled.

17
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.200
MQTT offers three delivery levels.

18
00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.010
Zero means at most once,

19
00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.510
with no acknowledgement.

20
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.190
One means at least once, so

21
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.610
duplicates are possible.

22
00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.530
Two means exactly once, at the

23
00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.450
cost of a four-step handshake.

24
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.660
Typical ranges in open country: Bluetooth

25
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.920
Low Energy tens of metres, Wi-Fi around a

26
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.100
hundred, Zigbee mesh a few hundred per

27
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.340
hop, and LoRa several kilometres, but at

28
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.160
only a few kilobits per second.

29
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:24.990
CoAP, defined in RFC seven two five two,

30
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.630
brings a web-like request and response

31
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.210
model to tiny devices over UDP.

32
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.800
It suits sleepy nodes that are

33
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.780
asked for readings, while MQTT

34
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.440
suits streams of events.

35
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.330
Protocol lectures become

36
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.750
lists of acronyms.

37
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.470
Instead, give teams a real brief with

38
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.710
distance, power and data needs.

39
00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.890
Make them choose and justify a

40
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.950
stack, then test it on hardware.

41
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:54.670
Quick check!

42
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.650
A sensor sends a temperature every

43
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.130
ten seconds, and an occasional lost

44
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.590
reading does not matter.

45
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.230
Which MQTT quality of service fits best?

46
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:07.350
Level zero.

47
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.650
The next reading arrives in ten seconds,

48
00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.070
so acknowledgements would only cost

49
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:13.430
energy.

50
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.360
Save higher levels for commands,

51
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.080
such as opening a valve.

52
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.430
In the app, open the mesh lab.

53
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.450
Place nodes across a field, remove

54
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.250
one, and watch the messages find a

55
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.370
new route to the gateway.

56
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:30.410
Your turn.

57
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.690
Forty hectares, no power, readings every

58
00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.990
fifteen minutes, a few bytes each.

59
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.150
Which link fits best?

60
00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.010
LoRa, with one gateway.

61
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.840
Remember: publish and subscribe

62
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.020
decouples devices, choose the lowest

63
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.740
quality of service that is safe, and

64
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.460
trade data rate for range.

65
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.080
Try it in your next lab: set up a

66
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.300
free MQTT broker on a laptop.

67
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.910
Each team publishes sensor

68
00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.190
readings to its own topic, and

69
00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.910
subscribes to another team's.
